# Normalize line endings. Text files are stored as LF in the repository and
# checked out as LF on every platform, whatever a developer's core.autocrlf
# says -- an attribute beats that setting, which is the point of stating it
# here rather than relying on everyone's git config.
#
# It matters more than usual for a Go repo: gofmt normalizes to LF, so a CRLF
# working tree makes `gofmt -l .` report every file as needing a rewrite while
# the repository holds them as LF and `git status` shows nothing. This repo is
# developed with core.autocrlf=true, so without this file that is the default
# outcome rather than an unlucky one.
* text=auto eol=lf

*.go   text eol=lf
*.mod  text eol=lf
*.sum  text eol=lf
*.md   text eol=lf
*.yml  text eol=lf
*.yaml text eol=lf

# The worker blob `wings build` generates. It never reaches a commit -- the
# scratch tree holding it is gitignored and removed -- but a line-ending pass
# over an executable would corrupt it silently rather than fail, so it is
# marked rather than left to auto-detect.
*.gz  binary
*.bin binary

# Windows-only scripts must keep CRLF if any are ever added.
*.bat text eol=crlf
*.cmd text eol=crlf
*.ps1 text eol=crlf
